1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,500
GRANDMA, WHY DOES EVERYTHING YOU MAKE TASTE LIKE SOAP?

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:05,700
JADA. WE TALKED ABOUT THIS.

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:07,400
TALKED ABOUT WHAT?

4
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:11,300
YOU'RE PUTTING ME IN A HOME?

5
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:14,600
MAMA. WE WERE GONNA WAIT TILL AFTER CAKE.

6
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:16,200
ON MY BIRTHDAY?

7
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:21,100
SOAP. YOU THINK I CAN'T RINSE A POT?

8
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:24,000
EVERY DINNER SINCE CHRISTMAS. WE'VE ALL NOTICED.

9
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:25,700
ALL OF YOU?

10
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:28,500
TASTE BUDS GO AS YOU GET OLDER.

11
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:33,400
BUT IF YOU CAN'T TASTE SOAP, WHAT HAPPENS WHEN YOU CAN'T SMELL GAS?

12
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:37,000
I'VE COOKED IN THIS KITCHEN FOR FORTY-TWO YEARS.

13
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,400
I KNOW. THAT'S WHY I DON'T WANT YOU IN IT ALONE.

14
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:46,000
WRENFIELD HAS A UNIT OPENING THIS MONTH. I PUT DOWN A DEPOSIT.

15
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:47,800
YOU ALREADY PAID?

16
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:50,800
NO, SHE WON'T FIGHT IT. SHE'S — SLIPPING.

17
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:55,500
GRANDMA, IT'S STILL THERE.

18
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:59,600
I SCRUBBED THAT PAN THREE TIMES. NEW SOAP. NEW SPONGE.

19
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:02,100
AND I CAN'T TASTE A THING.

20
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:04,500
JADA, AM I LOSING IT?

21
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:07,900
I LOVE YOU, GRANDMA.

22
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:14,500
WASN'T SHE THE ONE PUSHING YOU INTO WRENFIELD?

23
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:15,800
SHE SURE WAS.

24
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:19,800
NOW SHE'S HERE EVERY SUNDAY, TAKING PLATES HOME. WHAT CHANGED?

25
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:23,100
SHE TOLD ME MY COOKING TASTED LIKE SOAP.

26
00:01:24,100 --> 00:01:25,500
MINE TOO.

27
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:27,300
SHOW ME YOUR KITCHEN.

28
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:29,000
WHEN'D YOU BUY THESE?

29
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,000
AFTER THANKSGIVING. TERRANCE SENT ME AN ARTICLE ABOUT BLACK PLASTIC.

30
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,500
AND WHEN DID THE SOAP START?

31
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:37,800
CHRISTMAS.

32
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:39,000
SMELL IT.

33
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:40,800
THAT'S MY DISH SOAP.

34
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:43,600
IT SOAKS UP WHATEVER SOAP YOU USE.

35
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:46,800
THE MINUTE IT GETS HOT, OUT IT COMES.

36
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:48,900
BUT I CAN'T TASTE IT.

37
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:52,500
NEITHER COULD I. COOK'S ALWAYS THE LAST TO NOTICE.

38
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:56,700
THEY TESTED SILICONE BAKING PANS. IN THE OVEN, CHEMICALS LEACHED OUT.

39
00:01:56,700 --> 00:02:00,000
SOME OF THEM ARE LINKED TO HORMONE DISRUPTION.

40
00:02:01,900 --> 00:02:05,000
I THREW ALL MINE OUT AND GOT THESE.

41
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,200
WOOD? THE CHEAP ONES BLEED IN THE POT.

42
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:10,600
THAT'S THE FINISH, NOT THE WOOD.

43
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:15,300
EVERY PIECE IS ONE BLOCK OF WOOD. NO GLUE. NOTHING PAINTED ON.

44
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:18,900
THEY'RE CALLED ACAWOOD. I JUST TEXTED YOU THE LINK.

45
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:23,500
KEEP THESE TILL YOURS COME.

46
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:25,900
MOVERS COME SATURDAY AT NINE, MAMA.

47
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:27,200
SMELL THIS FIRST.

48
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:29,800
MAMA. THIS ISN'T ABOUT A SPATULA.

49
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:31,200
SMELL IT, TERRANCE.

50
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:33,800
THAT'S DISH SOAP.

51
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:37,100
NOW DOROTHY'S. SHE COOKS WITH IT EVERY DAY.

52
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:38,300
NOTHING.

53
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:40,800
ONE SNIFF. MY NOSE IS FINE.

54
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:44,000
YOU'RE THE ONE WHO SENT ME THAT ARTICLE.

55
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,600
SMELLING'S ONE THING, MAMA. TASTING'S ANOTHER.

56
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:50,200
FRIDAY NIGHT, I'M MAKING MY POTATO SALAD. EVERYBODY COMES.

57
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:55,900
IF ANYBODY TASTES SOAP, I'LL GET IN YOUR CAR SATURDAY AND NEVER SAY A WORD.

58
00:02:55,900 --> 00:02:57,200
DEAL.

59
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,100
JADA. YOU FIRST. YOU'RE THE HONEST ONE.

60
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:05,200
GRANDMA.

61
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:07,100
THERE'S NO SOAP. NONE.

62
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:10,700
MAMA. I'M SO SORRY.

63
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:14,100
UNPACK MY BOWLS, TERRANCE.

64
00:03:14,100 --> 00:03:15,200
SECONDS?

65
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,700
MY SON ALMOST MOVED ME OUT OF MY OWN KITCHEN. OVER THIS.

66
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:25,900
BEFORE ANYBODY TELLS YOU YOU'VE LOST YOUR TOUCH, SMELL YOUR SPATULA.

67
00:03:25,900 --> 00:03:29,800
IF IT SMELLS LIKE DISH SOAP, SO DOES YOUR DINNER.

68
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:35,500
AND IF IT'S YOUR WIFE'S COOKING, OR YOUR MAMA'S? SMELL HERS BEFORE YOU BLAME HER.

69
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:41,200
MINE ARE ACAWOOD NOW. SEVEN PIECES, EACH ONE CUT FROM A SINGLE BLOCK OF WOOD.

70
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:46,700
NOTHING ON THEM BUT OIL OR WAX. HERE'S ONE IN HOT WATER. STILL CLEAR.

71
00:03:46,700 --> 00:03:48,400
HAND WASH, DRY, DONE.

72
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:54,900
EIGHTY-NINE NINETY-FIVE WITH THE HOLDER. FREE SHIPPING IN THE U.S., AND NINETY-DAY RETURNS.

73
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:58,800
THE LINK'S RIGHT BELOW. AND MY KITCHEN? STILL MINE.

